@node CMacs AI Brigade
@chapter CMacs AI Brigade
@cindex AI brigade
@cindex agents
@cindex multi-agent orchestration

The brigade is not a feature so much as a @dfn{fabric}: the layer every
other cmacs subsystem---and every user's own code---lays on top of to get
AI capability, agent orchestration, and memory.

It ships out-of-the-box features (a memory index over your notes, an agent
dashboard, GenMail, deliverable generators, AI in imgedit and vidstudio),
but its primary deliverable is the @emph{extension surface}: exposing
something of your own to every agent is one form in your init, and that
one form lights it up on all three delivery paths at once.

@menu
* Brigade Overview::            What the fabric is and why.
* Brigade Requirements::        Configure flags and dependencies.
* Brigade Probing::             Detecting the feature at runtime.
* Brigade Extending::           Registering your own capabilities.
* Brigade Notifications::       Being told when it finishes; voice.
* Brigade Workers::             How an agent actually runs.
* Brigade Dashboard Display::   How the dashboard takes the screen.
* Brigade Composing::           Getting a task without knowing the format.
* Brigade Loopback::            Telling the conversation an agent is done.
* Brigade Memory Builds::       Indexing your notes without blocking.
* Brigade Scheduling::          Running a prompt on a cron schedule.
* Brigade GenMail::             Triaging the mail you already have.
@end menu

@node Brigade Overview
@section Overview

A registered capability becomes callable by:

@enumerate
@item
in-process HTTP agents, as a tool on the agent's executor;
@item
CLI agents (@command{claude-code}, @command{opencode}), as an MCP tool
reached through the @option{--mcp-relay} bridge, scoped by that agent's
capability token;
@item
external MCP clients, on cmacs's existing MCP server.
@end enumerate

Everything the shipped features use goes through the same public API you
get.  There are deliberately no private back doors: if a built-in needs a
hook, that hook is public.

@subsection Design principles

@table @asis
@item Nothing is hardcoded
Every path, model, budget, endpoint and backend is a @code{defcustom}.
Shipped values are @emph{defaults}, never assumptions.  An ERT test scans
the brigade sources and fails if a literal path, model id, or endpoint
appears outside a @code{defcustom} form.

@item Org is the plan, the dashboard is a view
A live Org buffer is the durable plan model, so org-agenda, org-clock,
refile, capture and git history all work on it for free.  C is
authoritative for @emph{runtime}; Org is authoritative for @emph{intent}.
The @code{TODO} keyword is a projection of runtime state and an input
channel for commands, never a stored value.

@item Agents run out of process
A @option{--gowl} cmacs instance @emph{is} the user's Wayland session.  An
agent that blocks or errors in-process takes the desktop with it, so
agents run in supervised children.

@item Capability scoping is real
Each agent gets a randomly minted token and a 0600 @file{.mcp.json} under
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}; the allowlist is enforced in C at the tool dispatch
point.  An allowlist implemented in Lisp is bypassable by any agent that
reaches @code{eval}, so it is not implemented there.
@end table

@node Brigade Requirements
@section Requirements

The subsystem is gated on @option{--with-cmacs-ai-brigade}, which
@emph{hard-requires} @option{--with-cmacs-ai}; @command{configure} errors
out otherwise, because the brigade drives ai-glib's provider and
tool-executor types directly.

@option{--with-cmacs-libreclaw} is optional and adds extra surfaces when
present.

@node Brigade Probing
@section Probing the build

@defun cmacs-brigade-available-p
Return non-@code{nil} when this cmacs was built with the brigade.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-capabilities
Return a plist of compile-time capabilities: @code{:libreclaw},
@code{:mcp} and @code{:f16c}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-capability key
Return one capability from that plist.  Returns @code{nil} when the
brigade is absent, so callers need not guard first.
@end defun

@code{IS-CMACS-AI-BRIGADE} (and the lower-case alias
@code{is-cmacs-ai-brigade}) is always bound, even in a build with the
feature off, so a user config can branch without a @code{void-variable}
error:

@example
(when IS-CMACS-AI-BRIGADE
  (require 'cmacs-brigade))
@end example

The @code{:f16c} capability reports only that the F16C vector-scan path
could be @emph{compiled}, not that this CPU has the instructions---that is
decided at runtime by @code{__builtin_cpu_supports}, with a scalar
fallback, so a single binary runs on hosts with and without it.

@node Brigade Extending
@section Extending the brigade

@code{cmacs-brigade-deftool} registers a plain Lisp function and publishes
it on all three surfaces:

@example
(cmacs-brigade-deftool weather-now
  "Current conditions for a place, as a short sentence."
  ((place string "City or 'lat,lon'")
   (units string "metric or imperial" :optional t :default "metric"))
  :group 'weather
  (my/fetch-weather place units))
@end example

Tool keywords are @code{:group} (allowlist vocabulary), @code{:destructive}
(excluded from read-only agents), @code{:confirm} (@code{nil}/@code{ask}/
@code{always}), @code{:async} (the body receives a @code{done} callback),
and @code{:timeout}.

The other registries are @code{cmacs-brigade-register-agent},
@code{-worker}, @code{-isolation}, @code{-memory-source},
@code{-deliverable}, @code{-panel}, @code{-context-provider},
@code{-approval-handler} and @code{cmacs-brigade-register-notifier}.

A memory source supplies @code{enumerate} and @code{read-chunk}, so a
Postgres table, a code tree or a Zotero library can be indexed into the
same vector store and every agent's @code{memory_search} covers it.

@defun cmacs-brigade-parse-json-object answer
Return the JSON object in @var{answer} as an alist, or @code{nil}.  The
object is located rather than assumed to be the whole reply: models wrap
JSON in prose and code fences however firmly they are asked not to, and a
reply that is 95%% the right answer should not be thrown away over the
other 5%%.  Useful in any tool that asks a model for structured data.
@end defun

@subsection Allowlists and the relay

An agent's tool list may name individual tools, groups, or @samp{*}.
Before a CLI agent is spawned that list is expanded by
@code{cmacs-brigade-allowlist-expand}: group names resolve to the tools
in them, while @samp{*} and any unrecognised name pass through.

Expansion happens in cmacs rather than in the relay because the relay
runs in its own process, spawned by the AI CLI, with an empty
registry---a group name reaching that side would match nothing,
silently.  Unrecognised names pass through because most of what an agent
uses (@code{project_read_file}, @code{memory_search}, @code{gowl_*}) is a
built-in MCP tool the brigade registry has never seen.

Two rules the relay enforces with no registry at all:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Tools named @code{ai_*} or @code{brigade_*} are refused
unconditionally, so an agent cannot spawn agents outside the
orchestrator's budget accounting.
@item
The @dfn{privileged set}---@code{eval}, @code{bash}, @code{shell},
@code{execute_command}, @code{send_keys}, the C-patching tools,
@code{crispy_eval}, @code{bacon_eval}---is unreachable through
@samp{*} or a group.  Granting one is a deliberate act: name it
outright or the agent does not get it.
@end itemize

@defun cmacs-brigade-tool-allowed-p allowlist tool-name
Return non-@code{nil} if @var{allowlist} authorises @var{tool-name}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-allowlist-expand allowlist
Resolve group names in @var{allowlist} to concrete tool names.
@end defun

@xref{Top,,, cmacs, the org manual}, file
@file{doc_org/cmacs/ai-brigade/extending.org}, for the full worked
example: a telephony @samp{Call For Me} feature that cmacs deliberately
does not implement, built end to end from a user's own init file.

@node Brigade Notifications
@section Notifications and voice

The expensive failure is not an agent finishing silently.  It is walking
away while one runs and coming back forty minutes after it finished
without knowing.  A notification fired at the moment of completion does
not recover those minutes, because you were not there to hear it.  So
there are three mechanisms:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Notify when it happens}---speak, play a sound, raise a desktop
notification, run a script, print a receipt, or anything you register.
@item
@strong{Escalate what is blocked.}  A finished agent costs you nothing by
waiting; one waiting on an answer is burning wall-clock doing nothing, so
it repeats at widening intervals until dealt with.
@item
@strong{Report what happened while you were gone.}  Events that fire
while you are away are held and delivered the moment you return---the
first command after a long idle.  This is the mechanism that closes the
gap, because it does not require you to have been present.
@end enumerate

@example
(cmacs-brigade-notify-mode 1)
@end example

@code{cmacs-brigade-notify-routes} maps each event kind
(@code{started}, @code{finished}, @code{failed}, @code{needs-input},
@code{budget-exceeded}, @code{all-done}, @code{away-quiet}) to the
notifiers that fire for it.  The shipped notifiers are @code{message},
@code{speak} (piper), @code{sound}, @code{desktop} (@command{notify-send}),
@code{command} (a script, given the event in its environment) and
@code{receipt}.  A notifier whose @code{:available} predicate returns
@code{nil} is skipped rather than erroring, and one that signals is caught
and logged so the rest of the route still fires.

@defun cmacs-brigade-register-notifier &rest plist
Register a way of getting the user's attention.  Keys: @code{:name},
@code{:notify} (called with the event plist), and optionally
@code{:available}.  The event carries @code{:kind}, @code{:task},
@code{:agent}, @code{:state}, @code{:text} and @code{:urgent}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-notify-status
Report what the brigade is doing right now, aloud when called
interactively.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-notify-pending
Return the tasks currently waiting on you.
@end defun

@subsection Voice

@code{cmacs-brigade-voice} adds speech in both directions, needing
whisper for input and piper for output.
@code{cmacs-brigade-voice-listen} is the primitive: it records one phrase
and calls back with the text.  It is deliberately not
@code{cmacs-whisper-dictate}, which is a streaming insert-as-you-talk
toggle---transcribing a phrase whole also transcribes it better, since a
sentence split across three-second windows loses the context that
disambiguates its middle.

@code{cmacs-brigade-voice-task} dictates a task: it is confirmed, then
appended to a plan as an ordinary org headline and adopted, because org
is authoritative for intent and a spoken task should be as editable and
git-tracked as a typed one.  @code{cmacs-brigade-voice-ask} answers four
spoken questions---what is running, what is waiting on me, what has this
cost, what has finished---matched by regexp rather than by a model call,
since the answers are already computed.

@code{cmacs-brigade-voice-setup-approvals} registers an approval handler
that reads a confirmation request aloud.  The answer is still typed:
approval is a synchronous gate, and blocking the editor to record a
spoken yes would stall whatever else is running---under @option{--gowl}
that is the compositor.  It is opt-in rather than automatic because
registering any approval handler replaces the built-in fallback, which
refuses when it cannot ask; a headless session must not have ``could not
ask'' become ``went ahead anyway'' merely because this file was loaded.

In the dashboard the voice commands live under @kbd{v}: @kbd{v t} to
dictate a task, @kbd{v q} to ask, @kbd{v s} to stop recording, @kbd{v ?}
to hear the status.

@xref{Top,,, cmacs, the org manual}, file
@file{doc_org/cmacs/ai-brigade/notifications.org}, for the full chapter.

@node Brigade Workers
@section Workers

A worker decides @emph{how} an agent runs.  Four ship---@code{inproc}
(the default), @code{claude-code}, @code{opencode} and @code{shell}---and
all four are registered through the public
@code{cmacs-brigade-register-worker}, so one you register yourself is
reached by exactly the same path.

@code{inproc} runs the tool loop inside cmacs against the provider's HTTP
API.  It is the default because it honours @code{:MODEL:} and the tool
allowlist directly: the executor is built from the allowlist, so the
agent cannot name a tool that was not installed.

The loop runs on a worker thread with its own @code{GMainContext}, and
reports back through the dispatch callback registry, so the editor is not
blocked---which matters more than usual under @option{--gowl}, where the
editor is the compositor.  The private context is the load-bearing part
and not an optimisation: without it the loop binds to the process-global
default context, which is the one Emacs itself iterates, and the main
thread ends up dispatching the provider's HTTP completions and running
the model's tool calls.  A @code{bash} tool then blocks the editor for as
long as the command takes.

An Elisp tool cannot run on that worker---Emacs's allocator is not
thread-safe---so the call is marshalled to the main thread and waited
for, bounded by @code{cmacs-ai-tool-call-timeout} (120 seconds).

@subsection Running a local model through a CLI worker

@code{claude-code} and @code{claude-tmux} accept a model spelled
@samp{ollama/NAME} and run it through Ollama as the transport rather than
through Anthropic.  The command becomes

@example
ollama launch claude --model NAME -- @var{the claude arguments}
@end example

with claude's own @option{--model} omitted, because Ollama supplies it.
With the cmacs provider prefix on the front that is a model of
@samp{claude-code/ollama/qwen3.5:9b}: the first component picks the
provider, and everything after it is the model string that provider
receives, so the @samp{ollama/} ai-glib looks for survives intact.

Both worker paths have to build that command and agree.  @code{inproc}
gets it from ai-glib (@file{ai-claude-launch.c}); the subprocess worker
builds the same argv itself, because it spawns the CLI directly and never
goes through ai-glib.  Inspect either with
@samp{ai -p claude-code -m ollama/NAME --dry-run "hi"}.

Two things this is not.  @samp{ollama/qwen3.5:9b} with no CLI prefix is
the plain Ollama provider talking HTTP to the server, with no CLI in the
picture, and it resolves to the @code{inproc} worker.  And
@samp{opencode/ollama/NAME} is opencode's own @samp{vendor/model}
spelling, passed through untouched---opencode has no launcher
indirection.

The claude CLI must still be installed.

@defopt cmacs-brigade-ollama-program
The launcher binary (@samp{ollama}).  @env{OLLAMA_PATH} overrides it,
matching what ai-glib does for the same case.
@end defopt

@subsection Where the tools run

An @code{inproc} agent's built-in tools---@code{bash}, @code{read},
@code{write}, @code{edit}, @code{glob}, @code{grep}, @code{ls}---resolve
relative paths against the task's directory and run commands there.  That
is the @code{:CWD:} property on the task, which @code{agent_spawn}
records from wherever the spawn came from, and which
@code{worktree}/@code{podman} isolation sets to the sandbox it prepared.

Without it they would use whatever directory Emacs is in, which is
wherever you last visited a file---so an agent told to work in one tree
would read, write and build in another.

@defun cmacs-ai-tools-set-working-directory executor directory
Point @var{executor}'s built-in tools at @var{directory}.  An absolute
path from the model is still used as given; @code{nil} restores the
default.  @code{cmacs-ai-call} takes the same thing as
@code{:directory}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-register-worker &rest plist
Keys: @code{:name}, @code{:start} (called with task id, agent, prompt,
cwd, env and endpoint; returns a plist describing the run), and
@code{:cancel} (called with the task id and that plist).  An unknown
worker fails the task with a message naming the registered ones.
@end defun

@node Brigade Dashboard Display
@section How the dashboard takes the screen

By default the dashboard fills the frame and restores your layout when
you quit with @kbd{q}: it is a wide table plus panels, and in half a
frame the columns wrap and it stops being glanceable.

Columns are anchored with @code{:align-to} display properties, not laid
out by padding with spaces.  A space is exactly one column wide and a
glyph is not necessarily anything of the sort: @samp{▶}, @samp{✔} and
@samp{✖} are East-Asian-Ambiguous, so @code{string-width} reports one
column while a graphical frame lays them out as two from the font's own
advance.  Padded rows therefore came out a column short for exactly the
states you notice, since a fresh task shows the narrow @samp{·} and
everything else shows a wide glyph---and padding could not fix it,
because the width it would pad by is the width that is wrong.

Rows are padded @emph{as well as} anchored: the padding makes the
buffer's plain text line up when it is copied out, the anchor makes the
display line up when the two disagree, and where they do the anchor
absorbs the difference.

@defopt cmacs-brigade-dashboard-display
@code{full-frame} (the default) takes the whole frame and gives the
layout back on quit; @code{same-window} reuses the selected window and
leaves the rest of the layout alone; @code{other-window} is Emacs's
default splitting behaviour.  None of the three ever creates a window,
and @code{display-buffer-alist} still applies.
@end defopt

@node Brigade Composing
@section Getting a task without knowing the format

Writing a task by hand means knowing that a plan is an org file, where
plans live, which properties mean what, and that @code{:MODEL:} is
spelled @code{provider/model}.  Four dashboard keys skip all of it, and
every one of them ends in the same transient, so you get a look at the
task before it exists.

@table @kbd
@item n
Describe the task in plain English in a small window at the bottom of
the frame, then @kbd{C-c C-c}.  What you wrote goes to a model, which
proposes the whole spec --- title, prompt, agent, provider, model,
tools, budget, working directory --- and the transient opens already
filled in.  Nothing is created by that call.
@item V
The same, spoken: records until @code{cmacs-brigade-voice-stop},
transcribes with whisper, drafts, and shows you the result.  Also on
@kbd{v n}.  A transcript is never turned straight into a run --- the
failure mode is an agent spending real money on a misheard instruction.
@item C
Clone the task under point.  The copy carries everything the original
@emph{said} --- agent, model, tools, budget, directory, prompt --- and
nothing it @emph{did}: its own id, and draft state.  A task with
per-task overrides runs under a derived agent
(@code{researcher@@b12c7a46}); the clone carries the base name, so the
overrides stay editable in the transient rather than frozen into a
definition belonging to the original.
@item x
The transient itself.  Also @kbd{M-x cmacs-brigade-compose}.  Inside it,
@kbd{e} opens the brigade's settings and @kbd{q} leaves, discarding what
was composed.
@end table

Names the drafting model invented for agents, tools or providers are
dropped before you see them: an invented agent is a task that fails at
start, and an invented tool is worse, because it looks like it worked.

@subsection What is read out of the request

Provider, model and agent are picked out of what you said @emph{before}
any model sees it, and they win over whatever the draft proposes.  Those
three are stated rather than inferred, so matching them against the
providers, model lists and agent registry that exist is exact, instant,
cannot invent a name, and survives a drafting call that fails.

A name counts as chosen when something introduces it---a cue word
(@samp{use}, @samp{with}, @samp{via}, @samp{run}, @samp{using} @dots{}),
a qualifier (@samp{agent}, @samp{model}, @samp{provider}), or both---or
when a qualifier follows it, as in ``the sonnet model''.  A bare mention
never counts: without that rule, asking about a vendor's pricing page
silently routed the task to that vendor.

The two halves are deliberately not equally eager.  Extraction may
over-read---``compare the sonnet model documentation'' sets the model
field, because that is exactly the shape of a choice---and it is visible
in the menu and one key to clear.  The prompt is the half that must not
be damaged, so it loses only a phrase that was @emph{introduced} as a
choice, and that sentence reaches the agent as written.

Whatever is read out is also removed from the prompt.  ``Run the command
pwd with Grok'' is an instruction plus a routing decision, and only the
first belongs in the brief; passing the second on asks grok to do
something about grok.  The removal uses the same cue rule as the
extraction, so it can only take out what became a field---in ``with
grok, summarise the grok pricing page'' the first is a choice and the
second is the subject, and only the choice goes.  The drafted path is
cleaned as well, because the model is told to leave the routing out and
regularly does not.

The headline is a short label, never the instruction restated---the
choices are stripped from it, as is the polite opening.  A title the
model proposes is used only when it is genuinely shorter than the prompt
and not a prefix of it; models restate the instruction as the title
constantly.

@subsection When the draft comes back the wrong shape

It usually does.  Asked to @emph{describe} a task, claude-code answered
with an @code{agent_spawn}-shaped object---@code{action}, @code{task}---and
once put a slug under @code{task} with the real brief under
@code{objective}.  So the parser accepts the aliases models actually use,
picks the most prose-like value for each field, and refuses a prompt that
is a bare identifier or shorter than the request it was meant to expand.

The CLI author runs in an empty scratch directory for the same reason:
started inside a project it picks up that project's @file{.mcp.json},
sees the brigade tools, and answers a request to describe a task by
spawning one.

What gets written is an ordinary org headline in an ordinary plan file,
adopted exactly as if you had typed it --- so a composed task is
editable, refilable and in git like every other one.

@defopt cmacs-brigade-compose-author-provider
@defoptx cmacs-brigade-compose-author-model
The provider and model asked to turn a request into a spec;
@code{claude-code} and @code{sonnet} by default.  A CLI provider because
it needs no API key configured before the feature works at all, and
Sonnet rather than something cheaper because this call decides which
agent runs and with what tools.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-compose-author-timeout
Seconds to wait for the draft before giving up on it (120).  On expiry
the request itself becomes the prompt and you fill in the rest.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-compose-entry
@code{window} (the default) opens a small buffer at the bottom, since a
task worth handing to an agent rarely fits on one line;
@code{minibuffer} reads one line instead.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-compose-default-agent
@defoptx cmacs-brigade-compose-default-model
What an empty menu starts on: @code{general} and
@code{claude-code/sonnet}.  An empty menu is not much use if the first
two things you do are always pick the same agent and the same model.

Both are only fallbacks.  An agent or provider named in your request,
proposed by the draft, or carried by a clone wins over them, and the
model default applies only when @emph{neither} a provider nor a model is
already set---so ``use grok'' keeps grok rather than quietly acquiring
the other half from here.  @code{nil} leaves the field empty, handing the
decision to the agent definition; a default naming an agent that is not
loaded is ignored rather than written into a plan that would fail at
start.

Distinct from @code{cmacs-brigade-default-model}, which is what an agent
@emph{definition} falls back to when its frontmatter names no model.
@end defopt

Every key has a command behind it, reachable with @kbd{M-x} anywhere:
@code{cmacs-brigade-compose}, @code{cmacs-brigade-compose-quick},
@code{cmacs-brigade-compose-voice}, @code{cmacs-brigade-compose-clone}
and @code{cmacs-brigade-compose-read-model}.

@defun cmacs-brigade-compose-set spec
Replace the composed state with @var{spec} and show the transient.  The
way in for filling the menu from your own code: a plist with any of
@code{:title}, @code{:prompt}, @code{:agent}, @code{:provider},
@code{:model}, @code{:tools}, @code{:budget}, @code{:cwd},
@code{:notify} and @code{:plan}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-compose-read-model &optional provider
Read a provider and one of its models, returning
@code{"provider/model"} or @code{nil}.  Never returns a half-built
@code{"provider/"}: the runner reads that as a model @emph{name} on the
default provider, so the half-answer would run the wrong thing.
@end defun

@defopt cmacs-brigade-compose-entry-height
Height in lines of the compose entry window (8).
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-compose-start-immediately
Whether creating from the transient also starts the task (nil).  Off by
default because the transient exists so you get a look at what is about
to run; turning it on makes @key{RET} mean go.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-compose-plan-file
The plan composed tasks are appended to; @file{compose.org} under
@code{cmacs-brigade-plan-directory} by default.
@end defopt

@defun cmacs-brigade-plan-read-task plan id
The intent fields of task @var{id} in @var{plan}, as a plist.  What a
human wrote, not what the runtime made of it---so a task whose per-task
overrides produced a derived agent reads back as the base agent it was
written with.  This is what a clone copies.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-plan-ensure-file file &optional title
Return @var{file}, creating it as an empty plan if absent.  The
@code{#+TODO:} line goes in at creation so a plan's keywords never
depend on the reader's global @code{org-todo-keywords}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-plan-append-task file spec
Append @var{spec} to plan @var{file} as a new task, adopt it, and return
its id.  @var{spec} is a plist of intent: @code{:title} and
@code{:prompt}, plus any of @code{:agent}, @code{:model},
@code{:budget}, @code{:tools}, @code{:isolation}, @code{:cwd} and
@code{:properties}.  The one place a task headline is composed, so its
shape on disk is decided once.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-compose-set spec
Replace the composed state with @var{spec} and show the transient.  The
way in for filling the menu from your own code.
@end defun

@node Brigade Loopback
@section Telling the conversation when an agent is done

A chat that spawns a subagent sits parked.  @code{agent_spawn} returns an
id immediately---a blocking spawn holds a turn open for minutes---so the
work finishes, nothing says so, and the conversation waits for a human to
come back and ask.  The model had everything it needed to carry on and no
way to know it was time.

A finished run now delivers a turn back into the conversation that asked
for it: @samp{[automatic] Subagent a17 (researcher) has finished with
state `done'.  Call agent_result @dots{}}.  The model collects the result
and continues.

A task acquires a target either implicitly---@code{agent_spawn} running
inside a conversation notices which one, because the chat layer executes
tools with its own buffer current---or explicitly, from the compose
transient's @kbd{N} key, which defaults to none.  Either way it lands in
a @code{:NOTIFY:} property, so it is visible in the plan and editable
like any other intent.

Both of the editor's conversation surfaces are offered: cmacs-ai chat
buffers, and libreclaw rooms (including cmacs-channel rooms, and so
Matrix and the bridge, since delivery goes through the room's own send
command rather than around it).  Neither is special-cased---each is a
@code{client} in the registry, so a surface of your own becomes a notify
target by registering @code{:current}, @code{:targets}, @code{:live-p},
@code{:ready-p} and @code{:deliver} with
@code{cmacs-brigade-register-client}.

They differ in one way.  @emph{Automatic} capture works for cmacs-ai
chat, whose tool loop runs in Elisp with the chat buffer current.  A
libreclaw agent runs its tool loop in C, with no room buffer current, so
a spawn it makes records no target---use @kbd{N}, or spawn from the room
buffer yourself.  Delivery into a libreclaw room works either way; it is
only the noticing that does not.

Delivery never happens mid-turn.  A message inserted while a reply is
streaming interleaves with the model's own output, and one inserted while
you have something half-typed in the compose region would send your draft
too.  In both cases it queues and goes out when the conversation is idle,
or is dropped once @code{cmacs-brigade-loopback-give-up-seconds} passes.
A closed chat is dropped at once, and each task notifies exactly once.

@defopt cmacs-brigade-loopback-enabled
Whether a finished agent tells the conversation that asked for it (t).
Tasks that never recorded a target are unaffected either way.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-loopback-message
The message delivered, taking the task id, agent name, final state and id
again.  It reports the state but not the output: results can be long, and
@code{agent_result} is a tool the model already has.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-loopback-retry-seconds
How often a queued message re-checks whether its target has gone idle
(5 seconds).
@end defopt

@defun cmacs-brigade-loopback-targets
Every live target across every registered client, as
@code{(ID . LABEL)}.  What the transient's @kbd{N} key lists.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-loopback-current-target
The target for the context this is called from, or @code{nil}.  What
@code{agent_spawn} uses to notice it is running inside a chat.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-loopback-deliver target text
Deliver @var{text} to @var{target}, queueing it if the target is busy.
Returns non-@code{nil} when it went out immediately.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-loopback-task-target task-id
The target @var{task-id} reports to, read from its @code{:NOTIFY:}
property, or @code{nil}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-register-client &rest plist
Register a conversational client that can be woken.  Keys: @code{:name},
@code{:deliver} (TARGET TEXT), @code{:targets}, @code{:live-p},
@code{:ready-p} and @code{:current}.  A target id is
@code{CLIENT:REST}---the prefix picks the client, the rest means whatever
that client wants.  @code{:current} answers ``is this being called from
inside you?'', which is how a spawn records its origin with no plumbing.

The shipped implementation covers @code{cmacs-ai} chat buffers; libreclaw
channels or a Matrix room register their own and are reached identically.
@end defun

@node Brigade Memory Builds
@section Indexing your notes without blocking

@code{cmacs-brigade-memory-build} returns immediately and Emacs stays
usable while it runs.  Nearly all the wall time goes on waiting for the
embedding server, and that wait happens on a process object rather than
inside a blocking call, so the main loop keeps running.

Nothing touches the live index until the build finishes: the new one is
assembled in a temporary and renamed into place at the end.  Cancelling,
a crash, a dead embedding server or quitting Emacs mid-build all leave
the index you already had.  One unreadable file is skipped and logged
rather than ending the run.

@table @code
@item cmacs-brigade-memory-build
Start it.  With a prefix argument, rebuild even when the manifest
already matches.
@item cmacs-brigade-memory-build-status
Report how far it has got.
@item cmacs-brigade-memory-build-cancel
Stop it; the existing index is left as it was.
@item cmacs-brigade-memory-build-sync
Start it and wait, pumping the event loop.  For batch use and tests,
where nothing else would ever drive the sentinels.
@end table

@defun cmacs-brigade-memory-build-progress
Return a plist describing the running build --- @code{:files},
@code{:nfiles}, @code{:chunks}, @code{:elapsed}, @code{:percent} --- or
@code{nil}.  The public view for anything that wants to display it; the
dashboard header uses this.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-memory-build-running-p
Whether an index build is in progress.
@end defun

@defopt cmacs-brigade-memory-build-scan-budget
Files examined per turn before control goes back to Emacs (200).  Only
files that produce no chunks at all reach it, but a run of thousands
would otherwise be one long uninterruptible scan.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-memory-build-report-interval
Seconds between progress reports (10).  Reports go through
@code{message}, so they accumulate in @file{*Messages*} whether or not
you were watching the echo area --- which is the point, since the build
outlasts your attention by some hours.  One landing while you are in the
minibuffer is written to @file{*Messages*} only.
@end defopt

@subsection Why not a thread

Because a thread does not fix this.  Emacs threads are cooperative: only
one runs Lisp at a time, and @code{call-process} is not a yield point, so
a thread sitting in it holds the global Lisp lock and the main thread
cannot run at all until it returns.  Measured here, one second of
main-thread work alongside a thread doing @code{(call-process "sleep" nil
nil nil "3")} took @strong{3.95 seconds}; alongside one waiting on a
process object it took @strong{1.00 seconds}.

So the fix has to make the embedding call asynchronous --- and once it
is, a thread adds nothing while still bringing a second Lisp execution
context into a subsystem that calls into GLib.  Under @option{--gowl} a
crash there takes the desktop session with it.

@node Brigade Scheduling
@section Scheduled runs

A schedule is a prompt, a provider and model, a tool allowlist, and a
time to fire.  When it fires it creates an ordinary brigade task, so
tools, budgets, isolation, the dashboard and notifications all work for a
scheduled run without knowing it was scheduled.

@example
(cmacs-brigade-schedule-mode 1)
@end example

Loading cmacs arms nothing: the tools and dashboard panel register at
load, but no schedule fires until the mode is on.

Schedules are org headlines, for the same reason plans are---greppable,
refilable, diffable, and visible to @code{org-agenda} for free.  The
headline is the description, the body is the prompt, the properties are
the schedule.

@example
* Weekday mail briefing                          :brigade_schedule:
  :PROPERTIES:
  :CRON:   0 8 * * 1-5
  :AGENT:  triage
  :MODEL:  claude/claude-sonnet-4-6
  :TOOLS:  mail_search, mail_read, memory_search
  :BUDGET: 0.25
  :END:
  Summarise anything that arrived overnight.
@end example

Recognised properties are @code{:CRON:} (required), @code{:AGENT:},
@code{:MODEL:}, @code{:TOOLS:}, @code{:BUDGET:}, @code{:ISOLATION:},
@code{:WORKER:}, @code{:CATCHUP:}, @code{:ENABLED:} and @code{:DESC:}.  A
headline without @code{:CRON:} is not a schedule---a schedule file is
still an org file, and prose headings in it stay prose.

Model and tool selection works by deriving an agent from the named one,
applying the overrides, and registering it through the ordinary
@code{cmacs-brigade-register-agent}.  A schedule can therefore express
exactly what a hand-written agent definition can, and nothing it could
not.

@subsection The cron dialect

Five fields---minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week---each
taking @samp{*}, a number, a list, a range, a step (@samp{*/15},
@samp{0-12/6}, @samp{5/6} meaning ``from 5 onwards''), or a name.
@samp{?} is a synonym for @samp{*}, and 0 and 7 are both Sunday.

Month and day names match as a prefix of at least three letters
(@samp{jan}, @samp{january}), and the whole word must be that
prefix---@samp{monkey} and @samp{jan1} are rejected rather than quietly
read as Monday and January.

Macros: @code{@@yearly}, @code{@@monthly}, @code{@@weekly},
@code{@@daily}, @code{@@hourly}, @code{@@reboot}, and
@code{@@every N} followed by @samp{s}, @samp{m}, @samp{h}, @samp{d} or
@samp{w} for intervals that do not fit a wall-clock grid.

@strong{The day-field trap.}  When both day fields are restricted, cron
matches @emph{either}---@samp{0 0 1 * mon} fires on the 1st @emph{and}
every Monday.  That surprises people, but it is what every other cron
does, and using intersection instead would make the expression fire
almost never.

@subsection Having the AI set one up

@code{M-x cmacs-brigade-schedule-from-prompt} takes a request in plain
words, asks a model to turn it into a cron expression and a prompt, and
shows what that would actually do---the expression in English plus its
next five firing times---before writing anything.  The confirmation is
the point: a model is good at cron and still occasionally puts the hour
in the minute field, and @samp{8 0 * * 1-5} runs at ten past midnight
forever without ever complaining.

Agents with the @code{schedule} tool group get @code{schedule_list},
@code{schedule_preview}, @code{schedule_create},
@code{schedule_set_enabled} and @code{schedule_delete}.
@code{schedule_preview} is unconfirmed so the model is not discouraged
from checking itself; the three that change things are
@code{:destructive} and confirmed, because creating a recurring job
commits the user to spend on a timer.

@subsection Missed runs

@code{:CATCHUP:} decides what happens to a fire missed while cmacs was
not running: @code{skip} (the default) forgets it, @code{run} fires it
once at startup.  @code{cmacs-brigade-schedule-catchup-grace} bounds it
either way, so a machine off for a month does not wake up and fire a
month of backlog.

@defun cmacs-brigade-schedule-describe cron
Return @var{cron} in English.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-schedule-next-runs cron n
Return the next @var{n} times @var{cron} fires.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-schedule-add title cron prompt &rest props
Append a schedule and return its id.  @var{props} may carry
@code{:agent}, @code{:model}, @code{:tools}, @code{:budget},
@code{:isolation}, @code{:worker}, @code{:catchup} and
@code{:description}.
@end defun

