@file{doc_org/cmacs/ai-brigade/scheduling.org}, for the full chapter.

@node Brigade GenMail
@section Triaging the mail you already have
@cindex genmail
@cindex mail triage

@code{cmacs-brigade-genmail-triage} sorts unread mail into buckets and
writes an org report you can act from.  It needs @command{mu} with an
index and nothing else: @command{mu} already indexed the mail and
@samp{mu find --format=sexp} hands it back as Lisp, so there is no
second indexer, no IMAP client and no MIME parser of our own.

@subsection The inbox, not the store

Triage reads unread mail @emph{in the inbox}.  Mail that has already
been filed is unread on purpose---an archive of things to read later, a
list folder nobody opens---and hauling it back out every morning buries
the mail that actually arrived.

The folders come from @samp{mu info maildirs}: every maildir whose last
component is an inbox, so one account and one INBOX per account both
work with no configuration.  A store with nothing inbox-shaped in it
falls back to @samp{maildir:/INBOX}, which may match nothing, but never
to the whole store: a morning with nothing to triage is a wrong answer
you can see, and a morning spent triaging the archive is one you cannot.

@defopt cmacs-brigade-genmail-inbox-query
The mu query selecting the folders triage and the briefing read.
@code{nil} derives it as above.
@end defopt

@subsection Rules first, then a model

A deterministic pass runs first.  A @code{List-Unsubscribe} header
identifies a newsletter with certainty, and spending a model call to
reach the same answer less reliably would be the most common thing this
code does and the least useful.  On a real inbox that classified 92 of
100 unread messages with no model call at all.

Whatever the rules cannot decide goes to the model in @emph{one} batched
call.  If the rules decide everything, no call happens---which is the
common case, and is indistinguishable from a broken model pass unless
you look at the @code{:BY:} property.  @kbd{C-u M-x
cmacs-brigade-genmail-triage} sends every message to the model instead,
which is how to prove the pass runs and how to second-guess a rule.

@code{:BY:} records how each message was decided: @code{rule},
@code{model}, @code{pending} (in the interim report only) or
@code{unclassified}, the last meaning the message was filed under
@code{reply} as a fallback because no model answer covered it.  A guess
is never recorded as a decision.

@defopt cmacs-brigade-genmail-triage-model
Provider and model, @samp{PROVIDER/MODEL} (@samp{ollama/qwen3.5:9b}).
Local models differ far more in speed and in whether they honour a
requested output form than their parameter counts suggest: on one
three-message batch qwen3.5:9b answered in the requested form in about
84 seconds where gemma4:12b produced nothing in 400.  Measure before
trusting a morning job to a different one.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-genmail-use-model
Whether to ask a model at all (@code{t}).  @code{nil} is rules only.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-brigade-genmail-triage-timeout
Seconds to wait before falling back to rules only (600).  For a job that
runs before you wake up, no report at all is the worst outcome.
@end defopt

@subsection The report arrives in two passes

The rules finish in milliseconds and a local model takes a minute or
more, so the report is written and opened straight away with everything
the rules decided, and rewrites itself when the answer lands.  Waiting
for the model before showing anything means staring at a minibuffer
message while finished work sits unwritten.

Undecided messages go in their own @samp{* pending} section, and the
header says what is outstanding.  When the answer arrives the file is
rewritten and a buffer already visiting it is reverted in place with
point kept---an answer landing while you read must not throw away where
you were.  A buffer with unsaved edits is left alone: notes you typed on
the mail outrank the rewrite.

The call is asynchronous for a harder reason than responsiveness, too.
Under @option{--gowl} the editor is the compositor, so a synchronous
model call would freeze the desktop for as long as the model took.

@subsection Following a link in the report

Each headline carries a @samp{[[mu4e:msgid:...]]} link.  mu4e's own
@file{mu4e-org} handles that type when it is loaded; genmail requires it
when it can and otherwise registers a handler of its own, because an
unregistered org link type is not an error.  Org falls through to a
heading search, so the link answers @samp{No match --- create this as a
new heading?} and the report becomes a list of things to go find by
hand.  mu4e is also not on the load path of an in-tree @command{src/emacs}
unless your init puts it there, so this is the common case.

The built-in view lands in @file{*genmail message*}: the headers worth
reading, then the message text, MIME decoded, the charset honoured, and
@code{text/html} rendered through shr when that is the only part on
offer.  @kbd{C-c C-r} opens the raw file, @kbd{q} buries it.

@defun cmacs-brigade-genmail-open-msgid msgid
Open the message with @var{msgid}, preferring mu4e.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-genmail-message-text path
The readable text of the message at @var{path}.  Distinct from
@code{cmacs-brigade-genmail-body}, which strips quoted passages and
signatures for the voice profile.  Triage prompts use this one: mail
that arrives now is HTML, and a raw excerpt is 400 characters of doctype
and quoted-printable with no signal to classify on.
@end defun

